@meta
id: 18-10
title: Go to the file under the cursor
chapter: 18
type: lesson
skills: edit-file, find-path
par: 34
minutes: 4
vscode: Ctrl+click the import path
open: src/domain/order/order.provider.ts

@brief
Error codes are being renamed to say which system they came from.
`REFUND_FAILED`, thrown by the payment service, becomes
`PAYMENT_REFUND_FAILED`. The provider you are in imports that service.

Put the cursor on the import path and press `gf`. Vim reads the file
name under the cursor, resolves it against the current file's
directory, tries the extensions in `suffixesadd` (the TypeScript
ftplugin sets `.ts` for you), and opens it. No `:find`, no `path`
needed for a relative import.

Rename the code in the service. The provider stays as it is.

@keys
| `gf` | go to the file named under the cursor |
| `Ctrl-W f` | the same, in a split |
| `Ctrl-O` | jump back to where you came from |
| `gF` | `gf`, then jump to the line number after the name |

@hints
The payment import is on line 4. Get the cursor anywhere on the quoted
path, not on the word `import`.
--
`3j` then `f'` puts you on the opening quote. `gf` from there works;
vim skips forward to the first file-name character.
--
`3jf'gf`, then `/REFUND_` Enter, `iPAYMENT_` Esc, `:wq`.

@solution
    3j f' gf /REFUND_<Enter> iPAYMENT_ <Esc> :wq<Enter>

`gf` is the fastest way between a file and the files it imports, and
you spend most of your day inside exactly that graph. `Ctrl-O` takes you
back, because `gf` is a jump like any other from chapter 17.

Where does it look? First the directory of the current file, because
`.` is in `path` by default, so `./order.repository` and
`../../infrastructure/services/payment.service` both resolve. Then the
rest of `path`. Bare module names like `tsyringe` will not resolve
unless you add `node_modules` to `path`, and you should not.

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
    };
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/refunds`, { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}

@expected src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/refunds`, { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('PAYMENT_REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}
